1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,328
Jump onto concrete,
and it jars your knees.

2
00:00:03,328 --> 00:00:06,119
Jump into a sand pit, and it feels soft.

3
00:00:06,519 --> 00:00:09,581
A fielder pulls his
hands back as he catches.

4
00:00:09,581 --> 00:00:11,283
Same ball, same speed.

5
00:00:11,283 --> 00:00:12,521
So what changes?

6
00:00:12,971 --> 00:00:14,701
Diya has a kitchen challenge.

7
00:00:14,701 --> 00:00:18,299
Catch a raw egg, tossed across
the room, without breaking it.

8
00:00:18,649 --> 00:00:20,556
Stiff hands, and it cracks.

9
00:00:20,556 --> 00:00:23,598
Hands that move back
with it, and it survives.

10
00:00:23,598 --> 00:00:24,498
Why?

11
00:00:24,585 --> 00:00:27,645
The egg must lose the
same momentum either way.

12
00:00:27,645 --> 00:00:29,301
The secret is the time.

13
00:00:29,951 --> 00:00:31,461
That idea has a name.

14
00:00:31,461 --> 00:00:32,361
Impulse.

15
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:36,484
Last episode, force was the
rate of change of momentum.

16
00:00:36,484 --> 00:00:39,395
F equals change in p over change in t.

17
00:00:39,795 --> 00:00:42,366
Multiply both sides by the time.

18
00:00:42,366 --> 00:00:44,948
F times delta t equals delta p.

19
00:00:45,348 --> 00:00:48,601
F times delta t is called the impulse, J.

20
00:00:48,601 --> 00:00:51,610
So impulse equals the change in momentum.

21
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:56,552
Its unit is the newton second, the
same as a kilogram metre per second.

22
00:00:57,202 --> 00:00:58,794
To the motion notebook.

23
00:00:58,794 --> 00:01:02,892
Two clay balls of two hundred
grams, at five metres per second.

24
00:01:02,892 --> 00:01:04,387
Clay does not bounce.

25
00:01:04,737 --> 00:01:08,827
The first hits a hard wall, and
stops in two hundredths of a second.

26
00:01:09,227 --> 00:01:13,542
In slow motion, the force shoots up
to almost eighty newtons, then falls

27
00:01:13,542 --> 00:01:14,442
away.

28
00:01:14,064 --> 00:01:15,499
A tall, thin spike.

29
00:01:15,899 --> 00:01:17,876
The second hits a soft cushion.

30
00:01:17,876 --> 00:01:20,704
It takes eight hundredths
of a second to stop.

31
00:01:21,104 --> 00:01:24,267
Now the force peaks at
only about twenty newtons.

32
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:25,673
A low, wide hump.

33
00:01:26,073 --> 00:01:27,691
Now compare the areas.

34
00:01:27,691 --> 00:01:29,626
Both are one newton second.

35
00:01:29,626 --> 00:01:31,927
Each ball lost the same momentum.

36
00:01:32,327 --> 00:01:35,609
Four times the time, a
quarter of the average force.

37
00:01:35,609 --> 00:01:38,216
Fifty newtons, against twelve and a half.

38
00:01:38,866 --> 00:01:40,699
Let's write it in small steps.

39
00:01:41,049 --> 00:01:42,014
Step one.

40
00:01:42,014 --> 00:01:45,581
The change in momentum
is final minus initial.

41
00:01:45,581 --> 00:01:48,148
Delta p equals m v minus m u.

42
00:01:48,898 --> 00:01:49,817
Step two.

43
00:01:49,817 --> 00:01:54,616
For a steady force, J equals F
delta t, which equals delta p.

44
00:01:55,366 --> 00:01:56,294
Step three.

45
00:01:56,294 --> 00:02:00,711
When the force changes, J is the
area under the force time graph.

46
00:02:01,461 --> 00:02:02,385
Step four.

47
00:02:02,385 --> 00:02:07,589
For a clay ball, delta p is zero
minus one, so minus one newton second.

48
00:02:07,589 --> 00:02:10,319
The minus means the force points back.

49
00:02:11,069 --> 00:02:12,090
Step five.

50
00:02:12,090 --> 00:02:15,179
Average force is delta p over delta t.

51
00:02:15,179 --> 00:02:18,608
Same delta p, longer time, smaller force.

52
00:02:19,358 --> 00:02:20,415
Remember this.

53
00:02:20,415 --> 00:02:22,638
Impulse equals change in momentum.

54
00:02:22,638 --> 00:02:25,374
Stretch the time, and the force shrinks.

55
00:02:26,074 --> 00:02:28,246
Side by side, with Diya's egg.

56
00:02:28,246 --> 00:02:30,981
Sixty grams, at five metres per second.

57
00:02:30,981 --> 00:02:33,977
It must lose zero point
three newton seconds.

58
00:02:34,377 --> 00:02:37,504
Stiff hands stop it in
one hundredth of a second.

59
00:02:37,504 --> 00:02:39,495
Thirty newtons, a sharp jolt.

60
00:02:39,895 --> 00:02:43,268
Soft hands move back, and
take a tenth of a second.

61
00:02:43,268 --> 00:02:44,575
Just three newtons.

62
00:02:45,175 --> 00:02:46,199
Now you try.

63
00:02:46,199 --> 00:02:49,161
Which catch gives the
egg the bigger impulse?

64
00:02:49,161 --> 00:02:50,433
Pause and decide.

65
00:02:53,633 --> 00:02:54,533
The same!

66
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:57,204
Both catches take away the same momentum.

67
00:02:57,204 --> 00:03:00,513
Only the force differs,
because the time differs.

68
00:03:01,163 --> 00:03:02,643
You see this everywhere.

69
00:03:02,993 --> 00:03:05,027
A long jumper lands in sand.

70
00:03:05,027 --> 00:03:07,050
Wrestlers fall on thick mats.

71
00:03:07,450 --> 00:03:11,778
Car airbags and crumple zones
stretch a crash over a longer time.

72
00:03:12,178 --> 00:03:14,517
And you bend your knees when you land.

73
00:03:14,517 --> 00:03:17,859
Each one makes delta t longer,
so the force is smaller.

74
00:03:18,459 --> 00:03:19,359
Your turn.

75
00:03:19,323 --> 00:03:22,610
Why does a high jumper
land on a thick foam bed?

76
00:03:22,610 --> 00:03:23,881
Pause and decide.

77
00:03:27,081 --> 00:03:28,657
A longer stopping time!

78
00:03:28,657 --> 00:03:30,887
The momentum to lose is the same.

79
00:03:30,887 --> 00:03:33,213
The foam spreads it over more time.

80
00:03:33,863 --> 00:03:35,598
Now, one worked example.

81
00:03:35,948 --> 00:03:40,345
A one hundred and fifty gram ball
reaches a fielder at twenty metres per

82
00:03:40,345 --> 00:03:41,245
second.

83
00:03:40,991 --> 00:03:43,566
He stops it in zero point one seconds.

84
00:03:43,566 --> 00:03:45,681
Find the impulse and the force.

85
00:03:46,181 --> 00:03:50,606
Delta p is zero, minus zero
point one five times twenty.

86
00:03:50,606 --> 00:03:52,743
Minus three newton seconds.

87
00:03:53,493 --> 00:03:57,761
So the impulse on the ball is three
newton seconds, against its motion.

88
00:03:58,511 --> 00:04:01,959
The average force is
three over zero point one.

89
00:04:01,959 --> 00:04:03,189
Thirty newtons.

90
00:04:03,939 --> 00:04:08,607
With stiff hands, in zero point zero
two seconds, it would be one hundred

91
00:04:08,607 --> 00:04:09,917
and fifty newtons.

92
00:04:10,567 --> 00:04:12,032
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:12,382 --> 00:04:17,004
A ball hits a wall at ten metres
per second, and bounces back at ten.

94
00:04:17,004 --> 00:04:19,482
Many say the momentum did not change.

95
00:04:19,882 --> 00:04:21,937
But velocity is a vector.

96
00:04:21,937 --> 00:04:24,048
Plus ten became minus ten.

97
00:04:24,048 --> 00:04:26,468
So delta p is minus twenty m.

98
00:04:26,468 --> 00:04:28,306
Its size is two m v.

99
00:04:28,956 --> 00:04:30,316
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:30,316 --> 00:04:33,739
In force time graphs, and in
bounce and catch questions.

101
00:04:34,339 --> 00:04:35,580
Exam level now.

102
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:40,407
A half kilogram ball falls from five
metres onto a floor, and bounces up

103
00:04:40,407 --> 00:04:42,353
to one point eight metres.

104
00:04:42,353 --> 00:04:45,213
The contact lasts zero point one seconds.

105
00:04:45,213 --> 00:04:46,465
Take g as ten.

106
00:04:46,465 --> 00:04:49,168
Find the average force from the floor.

107
00:04:49,168 --> 00:04:50,461
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:55,861 --> 00:04:57,203
The answer is C.

109
00:04:57,203 --> 00:04:58,691
Eighty five newtons.

110
00:04:59,191 --> 00:05:02,442
It hits the floor at ten
metres per second, down.

111
00:05:02,442 --> 00:05:04,067
It leaves at six, up.

112
00:05:04,067 --> 00:05:05,492
Take up as positive.

113
00:05:05,992 --> 00:05:09,182
Delta p is half, times
six minus minus ten.

114
00:05:09,182 --> 00:05:11,322
Eight newton seconds, upward.

115
00:05:11,822 --> 00:05:14,216
So the net force is eighty newtons.

116
00:05:14,216 --> 00:05:17,776
The floor must also hold up
the weight, five newtons.

117
00:05:17,776 --> 00:05:18,736
Eighty five.

118
00:05:19,336 --> 00:05:20,794
Here is your recap.

119
00:05:20,794 --> 00:05:22,294
Screenshot this card.

120
00:05:25,494 --> 00:05:27,424
Can you answer these without help?

121
00:05:27,824 --> 00:05:28,724
One.

122
00:05:28,284 --> 00:05:30,743
What is impulse, and what is its unit?

123
00:05:31,643 --> 00:05:32,543
Two.

124
00:05:32,124 --> 00:05:34,649
Why does a fielder pull his hands back?

125
00:05:35,549 --> 00:05:36,449
Three.

126
00:05:36,207 --> 00:05:39,040
A ball bounces back at the same speed.

127
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:40,990
Why is delta p not zero?

128
00:05:41,890 --> 00:05:44,634
Next time: two carts at rest push apart.

129
00:05:44,634 --> 00:05:46,971
Where does their momentum come from?

130
00:05:46,971 --> 00:05:48,997
Conservation of linear momentum.
